# Calibration analysis pipeline

Three scripts, run in order. Each reads the previous one's output; raw
capture files are never modified, so every stage is safe to re-run.

```
capture_calibration.py   ->  calibration_run_<timestamp>/     (raw, from the board)
filter_calibration.py    ->  <run>/filtered/                  (corrected + derived columns)
plot_calibration.py      ->  <run>/filtered/plots/*.png       (static figures)
make_replay.py           ->  <run>/filtered/replay.html       (animated attitude replay)
```

## Setup

```
pip install numpy pandas matplotlib pyserial
```

## 1. Filter

```
python filter_calibration.py calibration_run_20260729_112929
```

Applies two corrections and adds derived columns.

**Gyro bias.** The MPU6050 reads ~+0.42 dps at rest. Normal for MEMS, but
it integrates into fake rotation (~2 deg over a 5 s test). The bias is
measured automatically from the phase-A windows of the tests that start
from rest (`from=0.00V`) — the only windows where the platform is provably
undisturbed. Tests that spin up first are excluded, because their phase A
contains real leftover motion, including a genuine decaying oscillation in
the 3.0 V case.

**Sign convention.** Wheel velocity and gyro rate come out of the board
positively correlated, which would mean both bodies turning the same way.
They aren't: the encoder's positive direction and the IMU's positive
gyro-Z are just defined oppositely by how they're mounted. `--flip-gyro`
(on by default) negates the gyro so the two share a convention. After the
flip, momentum bookkeeping comes out right — e.g. test 1's wheel goes to
+7.7 rad/s while the platform goes to −12.6 deg.

Also uses `vel_filtered` (`motor.shaft_velocity`) as the canonical wheel
velocity and ignores `vel_raw` — SimpleFOC applies its `initFOC()`
alignment correction to the former, so it's the one that's physically
consistent.

Derived columns added: `t_s`, `phase` (A/B), `wheel_angle_rad`,
`gyro_dps` (corrected), `platform_deg` (integrated heading). A
`summary.csv` collects one row per test.

Options: `--no-flip-gyro`, `--bias <dps>`, `--outdir <path>`.

## 2. Plot

```
python plot_calibration.py calibration_run_20260729_112929/filtered
```

- `test<NN>_*.png` — per test: command, wheel velocity, platform rate +
  heading, all sharing a time axis with the phase A/B boundary marked.
- `overview_steps.png` — from-rest steps overlaid, aligned at the step.
- `overview_scaling.png` — steady-state wheel velocity and peak platform
  rate vs command. This is the one to look at before fitting anything: if
  the points sit near a straight line through the origin, a linear model is
  defensible.

## 3. Replay

```
python make_replay.py calibration_run_20260729_112929/filtered
```

One self-contained HTML file, no server or network needed. Top-down view
of the platform with the reaction wheel inside it, both turning from the
recorded data, plus telemetry readouts, a momentum-exchange bar, and
scrubable traces. Test selector switches between all nine runs.

The wheel's spokes alias at speed, like a strobed wheel — read the numeric
velocity instead. The momentum bars are relative within each body, not a
conserved total: `J_w` and `J_p` aren't measured yet, so the two channels
can't share units.

Options: `--out <path>`, `--frames <n>` (default 500 per test).

## Notes on the current data

The 2026-07-29 run came out clean: ~320 Hz realized log rate with under
100 µs of jitter, all nine tests ending on `platform_settled` rather than
hitting a buffer or timeout ceiling, and peak wheel velocity scaling
near-linearly with command (7.7–8.3 rad/s per volt across a 4× range).

One thing to carry forward: the 3.0 V hold shows the platform ringing
rather than settling quietly — a smooth decaying swing through zero in the
last ~175 ms of phase A, not sensor noise. Larger disturbances may need
more settle margin than the fixed hold currently gives them.